 "productionNotes": "Jaguar built 115,413 XJ-S and XJS cars between 1975 and 1996, and the sources consulted here agree on that figure. Wikipedia's tabulation splits it into 14,800 pre-HE cars, 73,207 HE, 3.6 and XJ-SC cars, and 27,406 facelifted cars, which sum exactly to the total; the Drives Today buyer's guide independently records the final car as number 115,413; and Ate Up With Motor puts lifetime sales at more than 115,000. One caveat stands: a final car number and a production count are the same thing only if numbering began at one and ran without gaps, and no source here addresses that. None of these sources publishes a US-only total. The annual figures Ate Up With Motor gives (949 cars in 1975, about 2,600 a year in 1976 and 1977, nearly 3,400 in 1978, more than 10,000 in both 1988 and 1989, more than 3,300 for 1996) are worldwide, not American. Variant counts are thinner and less settled. The four-speed manual V12, which Jaguar did not import to the US, went into 352 cars according to Ate Up With Motor and 356 according to Drives Today; no source here resolves the four-car gap. Wikipedia counts 5,013 XJ-SC cabriolets; Ate Up With Motor says about 2,000 Hess & Eisenhardt convertibles were built for the US before the factory convertible arrived. JaguarSport XJR-S output was 787 coupes and 50 convertibles for the world market per Wikipedia, of which Ate Up With Motor counts 104 US-specification cars, four of them early press cars, split equally between coupes and convertibles. The Jaguar Enthusiasts' Club model table lists 1,526 under number made for pre-HE cars without saying which markets that covers, so it is not used here.",
